To fix this issue, you can add a check to ensure that the input provided by the user is indeed an integer before attempting to parse it. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        try {
            int number = Integer.parseInt(input);
            System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid number.");
        }
    }
}
```

In this modified version of the code, we first use the `Scanner` to read the user's input as a string. We then use a `try-catch` block to attempt to parse the input as an integer. If the input is not a valid integer, the `NumberFormatException` will be caught, and the program will display an error message asking the user to enter a valid number.

This approach ensures that your program can handle invalid input gracefully, without crashing or producing unexpected behavior.

