Here's a simple Google Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue();

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ 'address': placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == 'OK') {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude); // Assuming you want to store the latitude in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude); // Assuming you want to store the longitude in column C
    } else {
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(""); // Assuming you want to clear the latitude value if the search fails
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(""); // Assuming you want to clear the longitude value if the search fails
    }
  });
}
```

To use this script, follow these steps:

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script."
3. Copy and paste the script into the script editor.
4. Save the script.
5. Go back to your Google Sheet.
6. Click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script" again.
7. This time, click on "Run" to execute the script.
8. The script will search for the place name in the first cell of column A and store the latitude and longitude in the corresponding cells of column B and column C, respectively.

Note that you'll need to enable the Google Maps API in your Google Cloud Platform project before you can use this script. You can find more information on how to do this here: https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/geocoding/introduction

Here's a PHP custom function "smart_sort_array" that accepts arguments "set" and "order":

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    // Sort elements of set by age, ordering by order parameter
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        if ($a['age'] === $b['age']) {
            return 0;
        }
        return ($order === 'asc') ? ($a['age'] - $b['age']) : ($b['age'] - $a['age']);
    });

    return $set;
}
```

To use this function, simply call it with your set and order parameters:

```php
$set = [
    ['name' => 'John', 'address' => '123 Main St', 'age' => 25],
    ['name' => 'Jane', 'address' => '456 Elm St', 'age' => 30],
    ['name' => 'Bob', 'address' => '789 Oak St', 'age' => 20]
];

$ordered_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'asc'); // Sort in ascending order
$ordered_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'desc'); // Sort in descending order

print_r($ordered_set);
```

JavaScript Promises are a way to handle asynchronous operations in a more organized and structured manner. They allow you to define a task that may take some time to complete, and then handle the result or any errors that may occur.

Here's a simple example of how to use Promises:

```javascript
// Define a function that takes a callback function
function fetchData(url, callback) {
  // Simulate an asynchronous operation (e.g., fetching data from an API)
  setTimeout(() => {
    const data = {
      name: 'John Doe',
      age: 30
    };
    callback(null, data);
  }, 1000);
}

// Create a Promise object from the fetchData function
const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  fetchData('https://api.example.com/data', (err, data) => {
    if (err) {
      reject(err);
    } else {
      resolve(data);
    }
  });
});

// Use the Promise object to handle the result or any errors
promise.then(data => console.log('Data fetched successfully:', data)).catch(err => console.error('Error fetching data:', err));
```

In this example, the `fetchData` function takes a callback function that is called with the data or an error object. The `new Promise` constructor creates a new Promise object that wraps the `fetchData` function. The `then` method is used to handle the result of the operation, while the `catch` method is used to handle any errors that may occur.

Promises are particularly useful when working with asynchronous operations that may take a long time to complete, such as fetching data from an API or performing a complex calculation. They allow you to write cleaner and more organized code, and they make it easier to handle errors and manage asynchronous operations.

