It seems like the issue is with the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header being set to `\*`, which allows requests from any origin. When you try to include an authorization header, the browser might not send it if it thinks the request is allowed from any origin.

To resolve this issue, you can either:

1. Set the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to a specific domain or list of domains that you trust. For example, if your application is hosted at `https://example.com`, you can set the header to `https://example.com`.
2. Use a more restrictive CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) configuration, such as setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to `https://example.com` and including the `Authorization` header in the list of allowed headers.

Here's an example of how you can set the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to a specific domain using Nginx:
```
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        root /var/www/html;
        index index.html index.htm;

        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin https://example.com;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Methods GET POST PUT DELETE;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Headers Authorization;
    }
}
```
In this example, the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header is set to `https://example.com`, and the `Authorization` header is included in the list of allowed headers.

