Here's an R code snippet that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
# Function to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric
can_convert_to_numeric <- function(x) {
  # Check if any element is NA
  if (any(is.na(x))) {
    return(FALSE)
  }

  # Try to convert each element to numeric
  tryCatch({
    x_numeric <- as.numeric(x)

    # Check if all elements were successfully converted
    if (all(!is.na(x_numeric))) {
      return(TRUE)
    } else {
      return(FALSE)
    }
  }, error = function(e) {
    return(FALSE)
  })
}

# Example usage
x <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", "5")
if (can_convert_to_numeric(x)) {
  print("All elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

This code defines a function `can_convert_to_numeric` that takes a character vector `x` as input and returns a logical value indicating whether all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric. The function first checks if any element in `x` is NA. If so, it returns FALSE. Otherwise, it tries to convert each element to numeric using the `as.numeric` function. If all elements are successfully converted, the function returns TRUE. If any element cannot be converted, the function returns FALSE. The function also handles errors that may occur during the conversion process using the `tryCatch` function.

Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and returns `false` if it's not equal to `true`. You can use this decorator to wrap around any middleware function you want to apply this logic to.

```typescript
import { createParamDecorator, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

export const IsProduction = createParamDecorator(
  (data: unknown, ctx: ExecutionContext) => {
    const request = ctx.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    return request.app.get('env') === 'production';
  },
);

export function ProductionMiddleware(middleware: Function) {
  return async (req, res, next) => {
    if (!IsProduction()) {
      return false;
    }

    await middleware(req, res, next);
  };
}
```

To use this decorator, simply wrap your middleware function with the `ProductionMiddleware` function:

```typescript
@UseGuards(ProductionMiddleware)
async someControllerAction(req, res, next) {
  // Your controller action code here
}
```

This will ensure that the middleware function is only executed when the `NODE_ENV` variable is set to `production`.

