To configure the boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage in your Chrome extension, follow these steps:

1. Create a background script (e.g., `background.js`) that will handle the injection of JavaScript.

```javascript
// background.js

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(
  (request, sender, sendResponse) => {
    if (request.action === 'injectScript') {
      chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, tabs => {
        chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, { file: request.script });
      });
    }
  }
);
```

2. In your content script (e.g., `content.js`), add an event listener to trigger the background script when the page is loaded.

```javascript
// content.js

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'injectScript', script: 'path/to/your/script.js' });
});
```

3. Replace `'path/to/your/script.js'` with the path to your actual JavaScript file that you want to inject into the webpage.

4. Update your manifest file (e.g., `manifest.json`) to include the necessary permissions and scripts.

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Your Extension Description",
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content.js"]
    }
  ],
  "background": {
    "scripts": ["background.js"]
  }
