To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple distance check before adding a new planet to the array. If the distance between the new planet and all existing planets is less than a certain threshold, you can generate a new random position and repeat the process until a valid position is found.

Here's an updated version of the `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to call this function with the appropriate minimum distance value based on your desired spacing between planets.

Here's a React utility function that removes widows (words or phrases that appear at the end of a line and are not connected to the rest of the sentence) from a given string:

```javascript
import { useMemo } from 'react';

const useRemoveWidows = () => {
  const removeWidows = useMemo(() => {
    return (text) => {
      // Split the text into an array of words
      const words = text.split(' ');

      // Initialize an empty array to store the modified text
      const modifiedText = [];

      // Iterate through the words array
      for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
        // Check if the current word is a widow
        if (i === words.length - 1 && !words[i].match(/[^\w\s]/)) {
          // If it is, add it to the modified text array with a space character
          modifiedText.push(words[i] + ' ');
        } else {
          // Otherwise, add it to the modified text array as is
          modifiedText.push(words[i]);
        }
      }

      // Join the modified text array into a single string
      return modifiedText.join(' ');
    };
  }, []);

  return { removeWidows };
};

export default useRemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility function in your React components, simply import it and call the `removeWidows` function with the text you want to modify:

```javascript
import { useRemoveWidows } from './use-remove-widows';

const MyComponent = () => {
  const { removeWidows } = useRemoveWidows();

  const text = 'This is a sample text with some widows at the end.';
  const modifiedText = removeWidows(text);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{modifiedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

